+### Enabling the provider
+
+Loading and activating the module registers nothing by itself.  A second
+switch, the provider parameter `enable-legacy-allowed`, defaults to `0`, and
+while it is off every operation query from the core is answered with an empty
+list.  For fetches this is indistinguishable from `activate = 0`; the
+difference is that the provider is loaded, activated and stays registered
+with the core, so `OSSL_PROVIDER_available()` reports it, `openssl list
+-providers` shows it, and its parameters can be queried.  Set it in the
+provider's configuration section:
+
+    [legacy_sect]
+    activate = 1
+    enable-legacy-allowed = 1
+
+or, for the command-line tools, after loading the provider:
+
+    openssl dgst -provider legacy -provparam legacy:enable-legacy-allowed=1 ...
+
+Programmatically, call `OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter(prov,
+"enable-legacy-allowed", "1")` on the loaded provider before the first fetch.
+The value is parsed like `enable-des` below, and has to be set before the
+first fetch for the same reason.  `test/legacy.cnf`,
+`test/default-and-legacy.cnf` and the `90-test_includes` data file that
+activate this provider all set it, so the test suite sees the full set.
+
 ### Enabling DES
 
 DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC are compiled in but not registered unless the
@@ -36,8 +63,13 @@ Programmatically, call `OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter(prov, "enable-des",
 through the core's `get_params` each time the cipher list is queried, using
 the same parser as the other providers: `1`, `yes`, `true` and `on` enable,
 `0`, `no`, `false` and `off` disable, anything else is treated as the default.
-Without it, `openssl list` shows seven algorithms from this provider; with
-it, nine.  A `no-des` build has nothing to enable and ignores the parameter.
+The core remembers which operations it has already asked a provider about,
+so a value set after the first fetch of that operation is not seen until the
+method store is flushed (for example by activating another provider).
+With `enable-legacy-allowed = 1` alone, `openssl list` shows seven algorithms
+from this provider; with `enable-des = 1` as well, nine.  `enable-des` has no
+effect while `enable-legacy-allowed` is off.  A `no-des` build has nothing to
+enable and ignores the parameter.
 
 ### Configure options that shrink the set further
 
@@ -178,19 +210,28 @@ The module should depend on nothing but the C library:
